WEBVTT

1
00:00:14.097 --> 00:00:16.319
the next few days, I saw little of Mr.

2
00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:23.345
Rochester. Sometimes, when he passed me in the hall or on the stairs, he barely acknowledged me, but

3
00:00:23.445 --> 00:00:25.207
other times he bowed and smiled.

4
00:00:26.228 --> 00:00:30.432
His moodiness didn't offend me because I knew that I was not to blame.

5
00:00:31.584 --> 00:00:37.506
One evening he had guests to dinner and sent for my portfolio, probably to show off its contents.

6
00:00:38.686 --> 00:00:42.708
When everyone had left, he requested that Adele and I come to the dining room.

7
00:00:42.728 --> 00:00:44.488
"My gift!

8
00:00:44.588 --> 00:00:45.268
My gift!"

9
00:00:45.749 --> 00:00:49.030
exclaimed Adele as soon as she spotted a box on the table.

10
00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:55.612
"Yes, there is your present at last," said the deep and rather sarcastic voice of Mr.

11
00:00:55.672 --> 00:01:01.757
Rochester. The voice came from the depths of an immense easy chair that faced away from us and

12
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:02.738
toward the fire.

13
00:01:02.758 --> 00:01:06.178
"Open it in silence and don't bother me."

14
00:01:07.939 --> 00:01:11.740
Adele lifted the lid and pulled out gifts wrapped in silver paper.

15
00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:14.421
"Oh, how beautiful!"

16
00:01:14.821 --> 00:01:15.841
she cried in French.

17
00:01:15.861 --> 00:01:18.222
"Is Miss Eyre there?"

18
00:01:19.322 --> 00:01:24.103
Mr. Rochester rose from his seat to look at me since I was still standing near the door.

19
00:01:24.123 --> 00:01:26.044
"Come sit here."

20
00:01:27.156 --> 00:01:29.097
He pulled another chair near his own.

21
00:01:29.117 --> 00:01:32.478
"I'm not fond of children's chatter.

22
00:01:33.539 --> 00:01:37.220
It would be intolerable to me to pass a whole evening alone with a brat."

23
00:01:38.381 --> 00:01:40.482
Mr. Rochester soon rang for Mrs.

24
00:01:40.522 --> 00:01:43.083
Fairfax and asked her to sit with Adele.

25
00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:44.923
After Mrs.

26
00:01:44.963 --> 00:01:48.825
Fairfax was settled, he stared at the fire, and I stared at him.

27
00:01:48.845 --> 00:01:53.247
"You examine me, Miss Eyre," he said after a few minutes.

28
00:01:53.267 --> 00:01:55.528
"Do you think I'm handsome?"

29
00:01:56.668 --> 00:02:00.311
I should have responded with a conventionally vague and polite reply.

30
00:02:02.133 --> 00:02:04.895
Instead I said, "No, sir."

31
00:02:04.915 --> 00:02:09.759
"Ah! There is indeed something unusual about you.

32
00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:12.301
Now what did you mean by that?"

33
00:02:12.321 --> 00:02:18.587
"Sir, I should have said that it's not easy to give a quick answer to questions about appearance,

34
00:02:19.147 --> 00:02:24.392
that tastes differ, that beauty is of little consequence—or something like that."

35
00:02:25.524 --> 00:02:27.085
"You should have said no such thing.

36
00:02:28.185 --> 00:02:30.206
Beauty is of little consequence indeed!

37
00:02:31.366 --> 00:02:33.527
You are no more pretty than I am handsome."

38
00:02:34.628 --> 00:02:38.449
He studied me as he rose from his chair and stood in front of the fireplace.

39
00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:40.870
"I'm in the mood to talk tonight.

40
00:02:41.891 --> 00:02:44.051
Speak." "What about, sir?"

41
00:02:44.072 --> 00:02:45.812
"Whatever you like."

42
00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:47.593
I said nothing.

43
00:02:48.633 --> 00:02:53.415
If he expected me to talk for the mere sake of talking and showing off, he had addressed

44
00:02:53.455 --> 00:02:54.976
himself to the wrong person.

45
00:02:56.128 --> 00:02:57.549
"You are silent, Miss Eyre."

46
00:02:59.230 --> 00:03:04.073
He bent his head a little toward me and with a single glance seemed to dive into my

47
00:03:04.353 --> 00:03:07.815
eyes. "You're also stubborn and annoyed."

48
00:03:08.915 --> 00:03:09.556
He paused.

49
00:03:09.576 --> 00:03:13.638
"I made my request in an absurd, almost insolent form.

50
00:03:13.678 --> 00:03:15.159
Miss Eyre, I beg your pardon.

51
00:03:16.219 --> 00:03:18.681
The fact is, I don't want to treat you like an inferior.

52
00:03:19.781 --> 00:03:24.324
I claim only such superiority because of our twenty years' difference in age and experience."

53
00:03:25.556 --> 00:03:29.818
He had decided to explain himself to me, so now I had to speak too.

54
00:03:29.838 --> 00:03:35.700
"I'm willing to amuse you, sir, but I don't know what topic will interest you," I said.

55
00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:41.683
"And I don't think you have a right to command me merely because you're older or because you've

56
00:03:41.703 --> 00:03:42.763
seen more of the world."

57
00:03:43.924 --> 00:03:47.985
"Nature meant me to be generally a good man, but you see I am not so.

58
00:03:49.826 --> 00:03:51.046
I can read that in your eyes.

59
00:03:52.187 --> 00:03:54.768
I'm not a villain, merely a commonplace sinner.

60
00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:59.122
But I have a right to get pleasure out of life, and I will get it, no matter

61
00:03:59.162 --> 00:03:59.922
what it costs."

62
00:04:01.003 --> 00:04:06.006
"Then you'll degenerate still more, sir," I said even though I had no idea what he might have

63
00:04:06.046 --> 00:04:10.748
done. He talked some more, and I still felt him to be a mystery.

64
00:04:10.768 --> 00:04:15.631
"In time I think you'll learn to act naturally with me," he said.

65
00:04:15.651 --> 00:04:19.453
"I find it impossible to behave conventionally with you."

66
00:04:20.634 --> 00:04:22.295
At that point the clock struck nine.

67
00:04:22.315 --> 00:04:26.041
"I must put Adele to bed," I said as I stood up.

68
00:04:27.181 --> 00:04:29.182
But Adele was not quite ready for bed.

69
00:04:30.283 --> 00:04:35.246
She had found something special in her box and now stood at the door wearing a pink silk

70
00:04:35.326 --> 00:04:39.208
dress. "Isn't my new dress beautiful?"

71
00:04:40.388 --> 00:04:43.950
She danced across the room and then dropped on one knee at Mr.

72
00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:44.951
Rochester's feet.

73
00:04:44.971 --> 00:04:47.592
"Isn't that how Mama used to do it?"

74
00:04:48.633 --> 00:04:54.824
"Precisely." Then he said to me, "I'll tell you her story one day, but for now good night."

75
00:04:56.025 --> 00:04:58.807
One afternoon he did indeed tell me Adele's story.

76
00:04:59.848 --> 00:05:02.910
While she played on the lawn with Pilot, we walked nearby.

77
00:05:03.991 --> 00:05:10.575
Mr. Rochester explained that Adele was the daughter of an opera dancer, Celine Varens, whom he had met

78
00:05:10.635 --> 00:05:15.979
in Paris He'd been in love with Celine, but she betrayed him with another man.

79
00:05:15.999 --> 00:05:22.664
"It's strange that I'm choosing to tell you this story," he mused, "and even stranger that you'd listen

80
00:05:22.704 --> 00:05:23.044
to it."

81
00:05:24.096 --> 00:05:28.960
He then said that Celine had claimed Adele was his child, but he'd never believed that.

82
00:05:30.801 --> 00:05:37.025
Years after he'd broken off with Celine, she abandoned Adele and ran away to Italy with another man.

83
00:05:38.146 --> 00:05:42.369
I realized then that Celine was not dead, as Adele had been led to believe!

84
00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:44.090
So Mr.

85
00:05:44.151 --> 00:05:47.133
Rochester brought Adele to England and asked Mrs.

86
00:05:47.173 --> 00:05:48.774
Fairfax to hire a governess.

87
00:05:49.915 --> 00:05:51.496
He stopped walking and turned to me.

88
00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:55.943
"Now that you know this, I suppose you'll be searching for another position."

89
00:05:57.004 --> 00:05:57.684
I shook my head.

90
00:05:57.704 --> 00:06:02.128
"She's a lonely little orphan who regards her governess as a friend."

91
00:06:02.148 --> 00:06:07.572
"So that is your attitude," he said abruptly as he looked up at the darkening sky.

92
00:06:07.592 --> 00:06:09.454
"It's getting late.

93
00:06:10.515 --> 00:06:11.235
Time to go in."

94
00:06:12.356 --> 00:06:16.559
That night I took Adele on my lap and let her talk for as long as she liked.

95
00:06:17.660 --> 00:06:20.663
When I had retired to bed, I reviewed the tale Mr.

96
00:06:20.723 --> 00:06:26.958
Rochester had told me For some weeks his conduct had been more uniform toward to me, and I

97
00:06:27.018 --> 00:06:30.099
sometimes felt he was more like a relative than an employer.

98
00:06:31.860 --> 00:06:32.520
And was Mr.

99
00:06:32.580 --> 00:06:34.701
Rochester still ugly in my eyes?

100
00:06:35.942 --> 00:06:36.682
No, reader.

101
00:06:37.823 --> 00:06:40.624
His face was now the object I most liked to see.

102
00:06:41.724 --> 00:06:43.725
Yet I had not forgotten his faults.

103
00:06:44.906 --> 00:06:49.888
He was proud and moody, but I believed his moodiness was due to some cruelty of fate.

104
00:06:51.048 --> 00:06:56.033
I can't deny that I grieved for his grief, whatever that was, and would have given much to

105
00:06:56.093 --> 00:06:56.733
assuage it.

106
00:06:57.794 --> 00:07:03.119
He had now been at Thornfield for eight weeks, a very long time for him, and I would

107
00:07:03.179 --> 00:07:04.220
miss him if he left.

108
00:07:05.321 --> 00:07:10.125
At last I fell asleep, but I was startled awake by the sound of something brushing against my

109
00:07:10.165 --> 00:07:12.187
door. "Who's there?"

110
00:07:12.547 --> 00:07:13.188
I called out.

111
00:07:14.349 --> 00:07:17.432
No one answered, and I felt cold with fear.
